#summary-page[
= In brief

vocabmod-se links the codes used in Swedish health data to the international terminologies that research and cross-border exchange rely on. It holds twenty-three Swedish code systems, among them ICD-10-SE for diagnoses, KVÅ for procedures, TLV's registers for medicines and consumables, NPU and six hospitals' local catalogues for laboratory tests, and ICF and KSI for functioning and social services. From these systems run 110,101 mappings to SNOMED CT, LOINC, ATC, RxNorm, and other reference terminologies, and to the concepts of the OMOP common data model that most observational health research uses. A data engineer can load Swedish codes into an OMOP database beside OHDSI's standard vocabularies, and a researcher or an agency can look up what a Swedish code corresponds to elsewhere. Everything is plain text in a public repository, checked against one schema, and a companion command-line tool exports it as OMOP vocabulary tables or FHIR ConceptMaps. The national classifications are carried as codes and hierarchies only, and licensed terminologies are referenced by code alone.

A Swedish code counts as covered when one of its mappings leads to a standard OMOP concept, directly or through one "Maps to" step. By that rule the mappings cover 58,260 of the 71,146 Swedish codes in scope (81.9%); most of the codes left uncovered are medical consumables and specialized laboratory properties, and review found no suitable standard concept for most of them. Every mapping carries a confidence tier or the reason why none could be assigned. 19,073 mappings (17%) are in the high tier, which agreed with a blinded, rubric-based review in 90% of cases (95% CI 78–96) on a held-out sample, so these mappings are reasonable candidates for lower priority in expert review; 45% of the mappings could not be assessed. The blinded reviews were made by language-model agents working to a written rubric. No domain expert has reviewed the mappings, and a tier grades the evidence behind a mapping, not its correctness.

vocabmod-se is an independent project that I build and publish on my own, part-time. The first mappings originate in GMLF, a Swedish emergency-care research project, where they were made as an exploratory step in assessing the project's data against OMOP, not as a research output in their own right, and they covered only part of the material. Everything since is my own work, done independently of that project: the extended mappings, the review that lifted coverage to its present level, the schema, and the tool. I have relied extensively on AI coding agents for the mappings, the tool, and this report; work of this extent would not otherwise have been feasible for one person working part-time. Mapping Swedish clinical code systems to international ones such as SNOMED CT and OMOP is a matter of broad national interest, and I publish the material so that others doing such work can build on what was found and learned. It is released as open source, not as a proposed standard: it offers the material gathered so far and one way such work could be structured. I make no commitment to long-term maintenance, but researchers and professionals are welcome to open issues, send pull requests, or start discussions, and a dedicated effort would be welcome to take the work further.

The corpus, this report, and the tool are published at #link("https://github.com/ludvigalden/vocabmod-se")[github.com/ludvigalden/vocabmod-se].
]
